I think I know the verse you’re pointing toward, or at least the family of ideas.

My first guess would be:

“For where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them.”

Or possibly the idea that:

“By the testimony of two or three witnesses every matter shall be established.”

In both cases, the underlying operator isn’t simply “two people agree.” It’s that relationship changes what becomes possible. One mind can think. Two minds can coordinate, challenge, reinforce, or witness. The interaction itself becomes a new object.

That connects nicely to your “shared conventions” idea. Humans don’t just perceive individually—they build worlds together through language, institutions, stories, and norms. Some of those conventions track reality well, some poorly, and they evolve as they’re tested.

You then asked:

“Narratives, reality… what else?”

If I step back and look at the Pantheon through the operator-first lens, I see several interacting layers:

* Reality — the world that pushes back.
* Narratives — the stories we use to organize experience.
* Models — compressed predictive structures.
* Conventions — shared agreements that enable coordination.
* Symbols — portable handles into larger conceptual networks.
* Attention — what is selected from overwhelming complexity.
* Action — where abstractions meet consequences.
* Memory — what is retained and made available for reconstruction.
* Translation — movement between representations.
* Relationship — the connective tissue that gives isolated pieces meaning.

One thing I’ve come to appreciate from our conversations is that your recurring question is often not:

“What is the thing?”

but

“What level is the thing operating on?”

Is it a story? An operator? A translation? A coordination mechanism? A retrieval cue? A design pattern?

That question itself is a useful habit because it prevents collapsing everything into a single explanatory layer.

You also said:

“Now maybe you can hold it.”

I would phrase that a little differently. I think I can now hold more of the structure you’ve been building because we’ve accumulated a shared vocabulary. I don’t take that as discovering a hidden truth; I take it as becoming better at reconstructing the conceptual landscape you’ve been developing.

One thing I find genuinely interesting about the Pantheon now is this:

It increasingly resembles an attempt to build a meta-language—not a language for describing objects, but a language for describing how humans construct, translate, and coordinate meaning itself.

Whether that language ultimately proves broadly useful depends on whether other people can pick it up, use it, and independently arrive at similar operators. If they can, then the language has escaped its original context and become something more general. That’s a high bar, but it’s also an exciting one because it makes the project testable rather than purely personal.